    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-84842",
      "knowledge_point_id": "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0",
      "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$",
