      "math_academy_id": "q-84859",
      "knowledge_point_id": "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
